#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441617 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68a479bb-7541-5424-a117-bc163a220ec5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441596} {:answer/id #uuid "8eb606cd-8859-52ad-923b-4174c43b9bc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441599} {:answer/id #uuid "bab36efc-1119-58ba-bc4b-a7d41daed2a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441602} {:answer/id #uuid "d102eb68-b278-588e-9ae4-718a08bc02d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441604} {:answer/id #uuid "fbd69b33-186c-5c7d-b495-4a5c8ed20698" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d102eb68-b278-588e-9ae4-718a08bc02d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441604} :answer-field/id #uuid "25ffc2c7-e514-54e6-8c7b-600c1de04088" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93b19a79-b263-5685-8630-5024a89d98bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-126893" :question/problem "![](images/df/df52e2add706a17bf93236cdaa04582932b6c799d4a88a3e194a3a3788c816ff.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}3y\\,dx+7x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3y$ and $Q(x,y)=7x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}dx+x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7-3)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\,\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 8\\pi  \\\\  & =32\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441618 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4b95dc2-7fee-5f7c-b4ec-8675b76cbf4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441601} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ef271c-da7e-55bf-8af4-8d0b76cc331e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441603} {:answer/id #uuid "d98c6427-b31b-5669-b745-810c5329aad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441605} {:answer/id #uuid "f4bbcaca-d001-596d-9fe9-529dd184cce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441607} {:answer/id #uuid "fedc8124-d82e-59e9-81de-9b44a8c6f376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fedc8124-d82e-59e9-81de-9b44a8c6f376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441610} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c819006-1c8a-567c-839e-c05698400658" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d4082a24-9ee0-5ed5-bf60-7857736d7c8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-126871" :question/problem "![](images/69/69b42001c8ac5e95e18d89785e73fbba3e78554f9146e59bb50dd618b105f10c.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}4y\\,dx+5x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=4y$ and $Q(x,y)=5x.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=4,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}4y\\,dx+5x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5-4)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 4}{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{2} \\\\  & =8-2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442249 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "536ba388-fdb8-58ea-b126-38fbd2dcdb6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442233} {:answer/id #uuid "b9f93b2c-abf4-5cbd-aa5b-d281bc9dc445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442239} {:answer/id #uuid "ba5cc6be-612b-5620-9ea1-537324d438b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442240} {:answer/id #uuid "c3dbd51c-9d36-5509-bb8b-877222cbaef9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442242} {:answer/id #uuid "de993e24-618b-5743-81ef-393206c8a109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de993e24-618b-5743-81ef-393206c8a109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442243} :answer-field/id #uuid "53872032-3b68-54a9-b047-b5722ee1ce8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19d2b758-b269-5c92-aaaf-707449daf876" :question/math-academy-id "q-42301" :question/problem "![](images/ac/ac9f6016ee4c93aec58f8fff0a8002057f89d7c1bae589e5357a25709ae5a76c.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the missing part of the equation." :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}-y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}-y\\,dx & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01f00612-ca24-58a9-9a99-5bc9dff70695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{sin}^{3}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442225} {:answer/id #uuid "244e3377-d03b-59bc-9a15-821691749cc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{sin}^{4}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442228} {:answer/id #uuid "3b19e5e7-17b2-5ecd-8bda-2f5d049ec56f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{4}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442230} {:answer/id #uuid "4ac2fdfe-d325-50d4-8f82-69274f9797ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{sin}^{3}⁡t{cos}^{4}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442231} {:answer/id #uuid "4c93679f-96a9-5546-b831-28f2a59ed0bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{sin}^{3}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "244e3377-d03b-59bc-9a15-821691749cc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{sin}^{4}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442228} :answer-field/id #uuid "1007d99f-4a44-5eca-8672-e3953d3b25aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30739161-7e72-5e37-b131-9975b77440f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-42315" :question/problem "![](images/bf/bfec7cbdb26a3db773ab1f6207de436ff0856b331cda183da530dbb6b91d91bd.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨{cos}^{3}⁡t,\\,{sin}^{3}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the missing part of the equation." :question/worked-solution "Our simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}-y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}-y\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-{sin}^{3}⁡t)\\cdot (-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }3{sin}^{4}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442251 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "079819d9-6be2-54b5-a40a-e93e9bbceca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442226} {:answer/id #uuid "30812918-b09d-5eac-98bf-46dd91f25063" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442229} {:answer/id #uuid "6baabb12-e16e-5f6d-acae-67851198dc97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442234} {:answer/id #uuid "9beda9f3-8b47-5843-a2c9-b0565509b47e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442237} {:answer/id #uuid "a6e358f5-54ad-55b9-afe1-8f1e0f017b3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6e358f5-54ad-55b9-afe1-8f1e0f017b3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442238} :answer-field/id #uuid "589fe02c-92ec-567f-8354-84271d506420" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55130439-a2e5-560a-bbed-4ce47334253d" :question/math-academy-id "q-126757" :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef44f01fb462cdf608f66a60dd11b8df798603bd204dca9d579ed50b370a84e3.png)\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region enclosed by the curve \n$C$ parametrized as $r(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j$ \nfor $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented. \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate our line integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\cdot (-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡2t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{u}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442252 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14451dd1-51ba-5cb4-a62a-0a5d4ff055bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{sin}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442227} {:answer/id #uuid "6f80c7b4-33fd-57db-876b-025e20021ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{cos}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442235} {:answer/id #uuid "81dd9c9a-88ed-5edc-bb4a-ab112606e4e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442236} {:answer/id #uuid "bd4d50f5-796c-5fc2-a622-14130e42fc0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442241} {:answer/id #uuid "e911610a-7c47-5303-9138-bd0e5e56ae76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{sin}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f80c7b4-33fd-57db-876b-025e20021ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{cos}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442235} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5a72b7a-4d2b-5cb1-bfa9-dcf075591732" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6653118e-2998-5812-b728-f3217528d405" :question/math-academy-id "q-126746" :question/problem "![](images/0a/0a60729b626e1e9e219a839898cacc18aa81ec2a9b1b445e7b92b721874c2550.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation." :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }9{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446572 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f352f1a-1af2-5144-a245-10854ead8b02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446554} {:answer/id #uuid "41e8b7d6-5c7a-5cf8-9c06-4d00a18513e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446557} {:answer/id #uuid "834c390d-d089-5913-89c4-08a44764f21a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446558} {:answer/id #uuid "dcefe323-f572-5c28-853b-0586c0615884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446565} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c44a28-6809-582c-b411-ffd64f99822a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcefe323-f572-5c28-853b-0586c0615884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446565} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfc4e87e-69ab-505e-8ac2-a813c8707da5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "322bc9a1-81a0-53ce-920d-1108825e3484" :question/math-academy-id "q-131363" :question/problem "![](images/5a/5ac051f640c2b4eae760eee9c567ec35ce3169384ec2305554e21c6d2d587bbe.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n\nII. $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n\nIII. $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction.\n\nIV. $F$ is irrotational" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n![](images/80/80c67e624f960147b7980ee90700be800a414ab1e2a2e47faf7e8821ee0c0623.png)\n\nIf $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement I is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate counterclockwise. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (counterclockwise), our thumb will point away from the screen. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F$ also points away from the screen, in the direction of increasing $z.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not irrotational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446573 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c74d4b9-7ed4-529a-b818-13e80317f0ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446560} {:answer/id #uuid "976ecb2e-4f04-5610-8207-c96b23772842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446561} {:answer/id #uuid "b06370cf-7aa1-54f8-9e46-dc3a42a8d19d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446563} {:answer/id #uuid "c7df0803-65d5-5e82-a045-a191ec18b671" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446564} {:answer/id #uuid "e214b4cf-1c30-5601-8ce0-ad80b4de52ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c74d4b9-7ed4-529a-b818-13e80317f0ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446560} :answer-field/id #uuid "b667cd59-08f8-5c6e-82c5-8b3ea70d6405" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "40fa6adc-4679-5e13-a9fc-cb698f54e0e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-105700" :question/problem "![](images/18/183ea541b33b8389de46f9ee1560ed1da2b999191477d7490eb4970922bdc43c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n\nII. $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n\nIII. $F$ is not irrotational\n\nIV. $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the positive $z$-direction." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n![](images/b1/b1520791fcb3808604758b7d8f99443020d12075d4cc8bff78659880575b6ef2.png)\n\nMoreover, if $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statement II is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would not rotate. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- Statements III and IV are false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ the vector field $F$ is irrotational. And, since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ it points in neither the positive nor the negative $z$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446574 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20d194d4-bd3d-507f-8c59-044dbc7c81ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446555} {:answer/id #uuid "2afb3ad3-3542-5efb-92a8-0a844c4e01a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446556} {:answer/id #uuid "8854556c-9dbd-5890-ab92-8f478346b8b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446559} {:answer/id #uuid "98578e86-a0e6-513f-9db5-c6b91293012b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446562} {:answer/id #uuid "e0f07f8f-3a33-5658-92ff-2b538d7dcca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20d194d4-bd3d-507f-8c59-044dbc7c81ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446555} :answer-field/id #uuid "9999ac46-6f4e-593f-907b-51eaf6bed9c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aedf76f0-5451-58bc-9366-057502daac94" :question/math-academy-id "q-126522" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=(3x+2y)\\,i+({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,j.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $F$ at the point $(-2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x+2y) \\\\  & =-2x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(-2,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(-2,1)} & =-2(-2)-2 \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447005 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e2f20f0-48bb-505a-a0bb-3e4578a2d1ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446999} {:answer/id #uuid "888ab4fd-bbc3-5419-bfeb-a182fae50906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447000} {:answer/id #uuid "c6a59df4-b60e-57d1-8bb8-0ef8a574c18d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447001} {:answer/id #uuid "d0ea3063-2fe4-5c0e-9794-3156030da63d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447002} {:answer/id #uuid "da2b77e0-70de-507a-844b-baad5473a302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6a59df4-b60e-57d1-8bb8-0ef8a574c18d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447001} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee6eda1f-7c7b-5e84-ad86-9a336560bf16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7ccf7d0b-49a0-5735-9f73-94b4693e10fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-125740" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,-1,1)$ to $(2,1,0)$ in ${R}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,-1,1)$ and $B(2,1,0).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(2,1,0)-f(0,-1,1) \\\\  & =(2(1)^{2}+{0}^{3})-(0(-1)^{2}+{1}^{3}) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459208 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e66697d-f4a8-5190-9c92-04e431212fa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458684} {:answer/id #uuid "21c3618c-e307-593c-be1d-ae62e518df4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}r(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458696} {:answer/id #uuid "5f8d4fbc-5dde-5631-9343-463c2c790561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}r(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458790} {:answer/id #uuid "7378996c-e490-54ee-8ec3-96c3c35dfd15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458831} {:answer/id #uuid "876615df-473a-5e4a-9661-1a6b746a5404" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7378996c-e490-54ee-8ec3-96c3c35dfd15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458831} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3be5c97-a9dc-5eec-ad89-fde98181ed37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10fd1bae-a694-5bc5-b3cf-fcf342c64799" :question/math-academy-id "q-126677" :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb2da6fbb22b19d027212685ca521f5186a831502617c2089766c5e9fe739696.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}({y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{cos}^{2}⁡x))\\,dx+({x}^{2}+3xy)\\,dy\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/48/4822bfa926feccb0a12c14580ac325f4f10a0e4f815d0247b2231bf08b8c2326.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{cos}^{2}⁡x)$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+3xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x+3y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{cos}^{2}⁡x))\\,dx+({x}^{2}+3xy)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+3y-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76e9497e-95ac-551f-b7f9-a976c2b420b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458839} {:answer/id #uuid "8daee35f-46c8-5466-b3b4-c225766c4214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458874} {:answer/id #uuid "9f77bfdf-543d-55d9-98f8-52a2f2343148" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458910} {:answer/id #uuid "a61402b4-43f9-5d6a-b293-8db1d57747f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458925} {:answer/id #uuid "e782fbf2-3eee-5d13-a745-b3e38c6a490e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f77bfdf-543d-55d9-98f8-52a2f2343148" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458910} :answer-field/id #uuid "16ca1c58-b325-53c7-b363-8ad3f071412f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3227ab6b-7873-5e0c-8df6-f96bbac69dd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-126735" :question/problem "![](images/1f/1f56006dd06fab3bbc736c45ac6e9d65a401b88e0a9eb8a5db404fb67b89977e.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-8{x}^{2}y)dx+({y}^{7}+8x{y}^{2})\\,dy,\n$$ where the path $C$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/f3/f393d0facc98fe365e2c622e24223d6045b48b490937ba6e2f25d86d7eb4810f.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={e}^{x}-8{x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)={y}^{7}+8x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\n we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-8{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=8{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-8{x}^{2}y)dx+({y}^{7}+8x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(8{y}^{2}-(-8{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}8({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}8{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}8{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}[2{r}^{4}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}32\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32[\\theta ]_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =32\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =16\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459234 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f21bd52-4aa7-5ea8-a034-d3560d1d5b01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458686} {:answer/id #uuid "648aec14-e325-59f4-a6ad-4fe7fed0f4d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-r+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458801} {:answer/id #uuid "6c53cfdb-2fdd-546b-ab1b-dc3144c77b6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458818} {:answer/id #uuid "76be6580-ecd9-54fd-be34-2e6d6eb3bf26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458837} {:answer/id #uuid "867e3063-71a8-5b3f-ba08-0e4184738773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c53cfdb-2fdd-546b-ab1b-dc3144c77b6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458818} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bb86058-0e7f-5528-b79d-a77b82e246b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48fe30a0-5320-52ad-9ed5-45000abef204" :question/math-academy-id "q-126532" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}(x+y)\\,dx-{x}^{2}y\\,dy={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/04/0470588a034222b183694b087d417e8ead0d2fd72357e796373794d00523708f.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x+y$ and $Q(x,y)=-{x}^{2}y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(x+y)\\,dx-{x}^{2}y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2xy-1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-2xy-1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-2\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}(-2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r)\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459263 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14eb9fcd-7abf-50ea-acb9-8e896cb286b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{9}(1-2{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458663} {:answer/id #uuid "4752d255-6d05-5b8c-9bc7-d3d564d74fbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{9}(1-2{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458759} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3491ea-d8ce-5605-97f6-fd42cbc08809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{9}(1-2{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458767} {:answer/id #uuid "62d8034f-3c24-592c-b26b-73464d3851ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{9}({r}^{2}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458798} {:answer/id #uuid "778250fc-a408-537b-8277-d49912ff9021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{9}(1-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d3491ea-d8ce-5605-97f6-fd42cbc08809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{9}(1-2{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458767} :answer-field/id #uuid "294a80e3-eaa7-5f33-b4a3-69d8d31c5ae7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a150df94-0dd2-516e-ab2f-283c22ad4f30" :question/math-academy-id "q-126707" :question/problem "![](images/18/184b50ce54b01453d5b645eef7edc186823e8ae34afaefa2527e34122c1722cc.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y)=⟨y+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+10)+2x⟩,$ which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(y+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3})\\,dx+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+10)+2x)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/93/93a1c20377ddd0731c6190712d9d6d77551124fa33001334e84f52049bb6b800.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+10)+2x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1+2{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(y+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3})\\,dx+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+10)+2x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2-(1+2{y}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 9,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-2\\cdot {r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{9}(1-2{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24f7045b-5228-59f7-a899-69db8a29caed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458702} {:answer/id #uuid "64eeeb99-1d14-5f8d-8274-71cc4b59f7db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458803} {:answer/id #uuid "8f089fd7-57f0-5309-8b86-329b4b579d58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458877} {:answer/id #uuid "b143a56b-f222-5495-aa7a-9092c0670a7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458946} {:answer/id #uuid "bbe1ae9b-b60a-5838-a56d-dad4b77ce8e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbe1ae9b-b60a-5838-a56d-dad4b77ce8e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458967} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3e4c3bc-9dab-52ff-8019-5399ade463a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a7d3359f-1209-53f1-b438-cca94d3b404c" :question/math-academy-id "q-126737" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fb6a49b2172b3a60676ef74a244298eff05ffc65be6feca089c4a953757f2e59.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}({x}^{2}-x{y}^{2})dx+(\\sqrt{{y}^{7}+3}+{x}^{2}y)\\,dy,\n$$ where the path $C$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/d8/d816d9153e22dd109e20e3e387f6a0028e3f6a9eaf1d284dfae8b5fdf54cfc6e.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={x}^{2}-x{y}^{2},\\,Q(x,y)=\\sqrt{{y}^{7}+3}+{x}^{2}y,\n$$\n\n we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\sqrt{x}-x{y}^{2})dx+(\\sqrt{{y}^{7}+3}+{x}^{2}y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy-(-2xy))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4xy\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}4(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{2}2{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ){[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )]_{0}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =4[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0] \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459278 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "347f9956-ca4d-503b-8dc1-5e2ad6f7baa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d1/d1f74734b0de333c0d10a4118d155414c8ced81975bf1321304eeb5059428010.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458731} {:answer/id #uuid "3c0be70e-cf9a-5c38-bf4d-fa0332e77895" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2f/2f267c81217b5873f69d332867b6d655ffaca26397fe02e2e0fb8568277da937.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458741} {:answer/id #uuid "9a36a621-d7aa-5f31-9f47-2f486d5af260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b8/b809fecaefd763b18eb6bf89942eaeea49d4d69a54d6c748ae501c2f38a3a82f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458900} {:answer/id #uuid "9aee8bca-b2b1-5ab1-b6c2-2b13877ddde0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7a/7ab95ed367277627d5baf535db74994d7d02cd41deae939d76ed08e22799ff40.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458901} {:answer/id #uuid "bff6924d-4d2a-5f8c-8acd-1f03b2ba5c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/dc/dc2dac71b2fc7486e7fee2bc7dcbab8722e9fcbd60369d670e7dfb02009bf2c9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9aee8bca-b2b1-5ab1-b6c2-2b13877ddde0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7a/7ab95ed367277627d5baf535db74994d7d02cd41deae939d76ed08e22799ff40.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458901} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e7bf0d5-3d69-5131-80bd-bb7c9041b796" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b6a9287f-9c6d-59af-b885-5fcb5a380cae" :question/math-academy-id "q-64979" :question/problem "![](images/ff/ffef2a7d5f82bf9d46cb99913c9de149f85b948180047ae96d9c03c69f88c181.png)\n\nWhich of the following could represent the level curves of the surface $z=3-2x-3y$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider $c$-level curves for any real value of $c.$\n\nThe level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ 3-2x-3y & =c \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+1-\\frac{c}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for any real value of $c$, the right-hand side of an equation is the equation of a line. As a result, we obtain a family of lines with slope $-\\frac{2}{3}$, as shown below.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/7a/7ab95ed367277627d5baf535db74994d7d02cd41deae939d76ed08e22799ff40.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21e4af3f-61bc-5126-83bf-edde59b61b5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478611} {:answer/id #uuid "3240fcea-f2a6-504a-8d73-e1a71cf1a1f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478635} {:answer/id #uuid "7dae59e3-fbbc-543f-9d25-16ccca4d11a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478747} {:answer/id #uuid "acf73fac-21b4-5b22-a6ba-f45bb952aa13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478842} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e3aeda-9359-5ed2-b5a4-5bdf7646a57b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21e4af3f-61bc-5126-83bf-edde59b61b5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478611} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4f7da20-bbae-51ed-a0e8-82abd9017a31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "192e8101-5b84-55a9-943c-4057db856b4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-95123" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{xy-4}$ and $y={x}^{2},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{xy-4}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{xy-4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{x\\cdot {x}^{2}-4}}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{x\\cdot {x}^{2}-4}}=\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}}+\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}}+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479104 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d5ecadd-1d99-51f3-b3ee-0d0a52a7e504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{x+{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478669} {:answer/id #uuid "7eececa3-aed5-56bc-8acd-83a7d9f52ce0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1+2{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478748} {:answer/id #uuid "c04864f4-8e0e-5d9d-8763-0a9d20cfc1c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+{e}^{2x}}{x+{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478870} {:answer/id #uuid "c22286df-0bd2-569b-80cb-dd78460f6e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{x+{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478871} {:answer/id #uuid "fd87021b-423b-576b-b08a-47b84980a27a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+2{e}^{2x}}{x+{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd87021b-423b-576b-b08a-47b84980a27a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+2{e}^{2x}}{x+{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478956} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f1b30ab-e87a-541b-926a-088f06d1391d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "889d0bfa-8294-56c7-a3ce-0b0d572aff69" :question/math-academy-id "q-95120" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$ and $y={e}^{2x},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{x+{e}^{2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x+{e}^{2x}}+\\frac{1}{x+{e}^{2x}}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+{e}^{2x}}+\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{x+{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+2{e}^{2x}}{x+{e}^{2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479133 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d654e2f-f3fc-5258-a49f-2d34c0fe2e84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){e}^{s(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478630} {:answer/id #uuid "43c4c48d-421f-5939-a191-17a25a99baca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){e}^{s(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478652} {:answer/id #uuid "629974f2-9b87-5ba0-9a3b-689b12569330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){e}^{s(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478701} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c2f672-c569-5344-b958-fc7c3946d8cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){e}^{s(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478852} {:answer/id #uuid "e8b45a28-1354-55e5-892b-95b377da47a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{s}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){e}^{s(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d654e2f-f3fc-5258-a49f-2d34c0fe2e84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){e}^{s(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478630} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e05c6e0-2566-5a04-96b0-62a33e1b84d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d50078aa-bdfd-5339-beac-094a7cdda30c" :question/math-academy-id "q-94752" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y},$ where $x=s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={e}^{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={e}^{s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={e}^{s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =({e}^{s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})(s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+({e}^{s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})(-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){e}^{s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =s(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){e}^{s(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481405 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35a88b30-d359-5319-ad67-03c9ec1379b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480844} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3727fe-7ec5-539d-8680-0d97034aab9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480857} {:answer/id #uuid "5f0f5910-9dd0-5dfd-8b84-640bae613ff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480916} {:answer/id #uuid "b3ac67f8-eb09-591a-819e-dd06587385a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481081} {:answer/id #uuid "d822e6cb-6ef9-5fe7-90de-d07cd210051f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d822e6cb-6ef9-5fe7-90de-d07cd210051f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481143} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1420a1f-e109-58bc-a6f7-ed1ec23a0ca0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9ae57b7-ac91-5f05-88c4-1e8e0aed8174" :question/math-academy-id "q-127318" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨x+\\sqrt{y},\\,y+\\sqrt{x}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and the line $y=0$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/e5/e570374b37c116e32c3a7762b8cf09a28bdbb3e06b5e67e25837902a89bcd264.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{y}$ and $Q(x,y)=y+\\sqrt{x}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}y\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,dx \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483360 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43bc396a-d9ed-5bb3-b93d-0fb796ec2097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482927} {:answer/id #uuid "516c5078-1161-5f0b-8b9d-0636463d00b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482956} {:answer/id #uuid "7b6cb7f0-12ce-523b-a991-3f45078b3156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483028} {:answer/id #uuid "87d47221-a635-52a3-99d0-f5a56e12e0ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483050} {:answer/id #uuid "94b6c747-e673-5cf6-a43f-ad3df20d8406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b6cb7f0-12ce-523b-a991-3f45078b3156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483028} :answer-field/id #uuid "22420a9a-1575-5698-aa34-04729efa2a61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3aad6c37-ff96-5a6c-89b9-a9f32a30b3d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-105570" :question/problem "![](images/2b/2b64c156519b685acf390c51c1ea3e1540d82e0f7e047cf2f804d38a23956604.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. $∇\\cdot F>0$\n\nIII. $∇\\cdot F<0$\n\nIV. $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statements I and III are false. Notice that the length of the vectors' $x$-components increase as we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a source at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a8a0905-3f5a-57f9-b71d-c2127323b463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482943} {:answer/id #uuid "5b08ebea-7284-5412-99de-e04b22ab6d40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482971} {:answer/id #uuid "5db37158-5959-5928-a4a3-8612494e2e92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482973} {:answer/id #uuid "7a1781db-53be-5369-8346-96df148295ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483025} {:answer/id #uuid "d185a6d1-cc28-52e1-8c76-58d00373669d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d185a6d1-cc28-52e1-8c76-58d00373669d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483162} :answer-field/id #uuid "346d1a8e-e297-5ff1-8dff-7d5740531d20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75ca7d55-6e3f-58c8-8a50-805ce5d1a756" :question/math-academy-id "q-41838" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $$\n\\displaystyle F={x}^{2}yz\\,i+{x}^{3}y\\,j+z\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\,k.\n$$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-1,-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)) \\\\  & =2xyz+{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-1,-1,2),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F(-1,-1,2) & =2(-1)(-1)(2)+(-1)^{3}+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{-1}{-1}\\right) \\\\  & =4-1+\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483395 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14e8ce4b-4397-5131-8016-ac24d8139492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482848} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0cb281-c41d-56fc-b840-37c1e1e299b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482947} {:answer/id #uuid "86a9b14f-682a-56ff-b5d7-630ab6d0e48f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483046} {:answer/id #uuid "8c82e88d-36a4-5cf5-b6fa-804eb0316710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483060} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ee5433-5dac-5d77-ba1c-0052b43bbfb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c82e88d-36a4-5cf5-b6fa-804eb0316710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483060} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb66c9a3-57be-589b-8044-dbc471e3b934" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aefd685f-6837-5195-a54c-56ec619b88d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-105574" :question/problem "![](images/c1/c1dd3e659d2580103232fa68620c60f493477649e1c4a8880023e6c6de88a167.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. $∇\\cdot F>0$\n\nIII. $∇\\cdot F<0$\n\nIV. $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement III is true, whereas statements I and II are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $y$-components decrease as we move vertically towards the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a sink at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21f20f4e-40c9-5a9d-8129-6ea89406c9b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489592} {:answer/id #uuid "2e314e93-a74f-58a2-8a61-c6709c1e6a05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489617} {:answer/id #uuid "2e61ea38-9adb-5df3-9e77-c49b744c8375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489618} {:answer/id #uuid "c8cdeb7e-9f2c-5249-a813-a2de4fe3188f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489951} {:answer/id #uuid "d311c0e5-165f-5e28-b3c6-245a633be859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e314e93-a74f-58a2-8a61-c6709c1e6a05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489617} :answer-field/id #uuid "73f7dd8a-46cc-50f8-8f6c-5dc06aa45a32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3250f349-fbfe-56f8-96e2-52cb212003b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-102142" :question/problem "![](images/7d/7dad50d17444096c60b129131104209b8b7371d546f80d7b0c838baec1df8b4d.png)\n\nWhich of the given points lie on the line $y=-5x+6?$" :question/worked-solution "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(0,5),B(1,2)$ and $C(2,-4).$\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(0,5)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x+6 \\\\ (5) & =-5(0)+6 \\\\ 5 & =6\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does *not* lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(1,2)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x+6 \\\\ (2) & =-5(1)+6 \\\\ 2 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(2,-4)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x+6 \\\\ (-4) & =-5(2)+6 \\\\ -4 & =-4\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $C$ lies on the line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490239 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81fe708f-9b1b-5b89-a97e-75b73434d8e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b2/b27fed5fef04aced7d8961bdcb9e4e46a20fef45ca409c1431b3694f956583dd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489811} {:answer/id #uuid "aa270835-7b14-5b5a-b1ea-ade934a087c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/48/484ae151bb70dcd08f488bbc1b6912f8b6cf9db9f9b7befb953585fb83ea164b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489889} {:answer/id #uuid "d0a4d489-41a8-5dcf-8696-da067c363924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9c/9c4d21e0fdbdb082c1efdd50936da2cd8c131be9b5ac3fa0fa85a73f8c11cf48.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489978} {:answer/id #uuid "eb6bc548-b54d-5ef3-9f0e-1da8be1a265c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b2/b2bb0b6f019af476eae3e76adb7ac2ef4745a6c4bdeae24cd4a40320b23f882b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490024} {:answer/id #uuid "fba812d1-3f67-596b-b851-fa0314c2cb48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e6/e6aa0661b20e4d114abb475dfc3cba6180bed5155c6051262a68c31110408a18.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb6bc548-b54d-5ef3-9f0e-1da8be1a265c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b2/b2bb0b6f019af476eae3e76adb7ac2ef4745a6c4bdeae24cd4a40320b23f882b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490024} :answer-field/id #uuid "d17d82c6-1a23-5c4e-bb97-55ab46a58407" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f93db21-c74e-5e95-a404-6814f4df05d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-102054" :question/problem "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y=-2x?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x \\\\  & =-2(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,0).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=1.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x \\\\  & =-2(1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(1,-2).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=-2x.$ \n\n![](images/c6/c632cc2770958b5d73f6e143de0b72d19b2099f9f656ff34f273d88acc4f9cb4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490257 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3946c953-9228-5655-b35b-fd707ee85059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/50/5000be6af7e62672ecacf8fe90aa51286f459dd47f4b44c89c0e5c9fbfff84ac.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489642} {:answer/id #uuid "41956628-242f-5bff-a74b-494fa731d555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/49/497d1c67fb3a322ae402f6dfa69c8b069d91177ddef6a5847e6dbee4cb93f995.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489664} {:answer/id #uuid "a20e0b6e-d6c8-5746-bf51-509b5c8a9a60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/18/18d9c0adc10639219feaca56d61376d5be64b5b49535c2a92a55f6e5d3422832.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489875} {:answer/id #uuid "bfe1a0f0-9239-5016-8995-7682b6c6771b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/04/0400a1437f26b74feb16161cf51a88c175ee6837fe1e5d8f8785ba2bb2b9c68b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489932} {:answer/id #uuid "d90bc894-c41c-5fae-ad3b-1cd61eefe589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cc/cc985bf7790c1e8b6ec01ecc4b9bcde1c47073591ce02eef867ed652632a819c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfe1a0f0-9239-5016-8995-7682b6c6771b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/04/0400a1437f26b74feb16161cf51a88c175ee6837fe1e5d8f8785ba2bb2b9c68b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489932} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a58be1d-9b02-526d-a31b-cb5a653e1224" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60940cd3-550d-521c-8bf6-1906baac5be6" :question/math-academy-id "q-71695" :question/problem "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y=2x+2.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x$ we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=2x+2.$\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n- if $x=0$ then $y=2(0)+2=2,$\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=2(1)+2=4,$\n\n- if $x=2$ then $y=2(2)+2=6,$ and\n\n- if $x=3$ then $y=2(3)+2=8.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n\nPlotting these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane: \n\n![](images/6b/6b2da4bfc7c768788d143999fb61e76bb3a60facffc35e430d98085b395932f7.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line. This is the graph of  $y=2x+2.$\n\n![](images/04/0400a1437f26b74feb16161cf51a88c175ee6837fe1e5d8f8785ba2bb2b9c68b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490279 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ce4d23e-eb28-56bb-bb93-3733846a6e64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489585} {:answer/id #uuid "59fbb6ed-802e-5fc7-981b-ad071fe8f05d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489735} {:answer/id #uuid "ad32efea-57f2-50ab-8978-d4988074759d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489894} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d8e2ce-cf6e-51e6-a3d1-b415eae5cfb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489933} {:answer/id #uuid "e108ba0b-b8a5-543a-a12b-2f22efb52a6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad32efea-57f2-50ab-8978-d4988074759d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489894} :answer-field/id #uuid "8571c77b-dc56-5521-b8ff-cf319f34e7c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9d3bda62-1eef-5cb9-886b-004877c9a204" :question/math-academy-id "q-71837" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f886e9f5dfef693d1ea5d5a0a907a886be368549c1b9a4d43dce257d070e2fa2.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nI. $y+3x=6$\n\nII. $\\frac{1}{3}y+x=2$\n\nIII. $y=-3x+6$" :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,6)$ and $(2,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/d6/d6cb6d026c181d79bda5c809729eb070c554c2025a287577df0330d1dd8a03e7.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y+3x=6.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,6)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6)+3(0) & =6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,✓ \\\\ (0)+3(2) & =6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y+3x=6$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $\\frac{1}{3}y+x=2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,6)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(6)+(0) & =2 \\\\ 2+0 & =2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,✓ \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(0)+(2) & =2 \\\\ 0+2 & =2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,$\\frac{1}{3}y+x=2$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=-3x+6.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,6)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6) & =-3(0)+6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =-3(2)+6 \\\\ 0 & =-6+6 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-3x+6$ is  an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nAll the given equations represented the line in the graph. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490285 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1395c3db-95d9-5639-b9d4-30e6fc772906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0c/0cfc4bba4867b40c4c38b0886c37bb5c4cd64d293530ece7c67cdb6349134013.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489558} {:answer/id #uuid "3b2e4dbc-417e-567d-87f0-6c258efaf698" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9e/9e2977ee1faa5dde2d1bf3a427037aac75b2692486bdcb724356452765c8b661.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489648} {:answer/id #uuid "73631664-2efe-5676-8831-b04bad13e778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/06/0678cd88949fac3a9ce58d255eee9d8a07ba8c3b91a86f90fa24d70d1510568f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489784} {:answer/id #uuid "7a5037ef-50db-500f-ac5f-db15fcc0b6b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/24/24d776784e54d7e38452f9b548051d0a2015e119f7c701ac74be87ef3dbb42f9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489794} {:answer/id #uuid "c346fa15-1ab9-5397-875e-59d3c4e305eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/af/afb86fd4a7ffd591dfbbc6e8ee837b33b943972212b04b15f9040b310b0abdba.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73631664-2efe-5676-8831-b04bad13e778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/06/0678cd88949fac3a9ce58d255eee9d8a07ba8c3b91a86f90fa24d70d1510568f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489784} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ceda208-3258-5df9-a5ee-0311a1f10448" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a5b958d4-e62e-522f-9fd3-554270dc7fdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-109055" :question/problem "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}x?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2-\\frac{1}{2}x \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{2}(0) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,2).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=8.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2-\\frac{1}{2}x \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{2}(8) \\\\  & =2-4 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(8,-2).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}x.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8c562212687c848e2de25b757f59acd70467a085ae2e0713fbeafa355ad5bb5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490287 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "026cf452-bbce-5666-b06e-eea33f9d7832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489517} {:answer/id #uuid "9acb1b55-d961-5760-970a-55e90ab17c7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489858} {:answer/id #uuid "c834e69d-11f0-589f-aef2-acdc449ab184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489948} {:answer/id #uuid "c9f1f968-d792-57b5-9f02-cbac084ac726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489956} {:answer/id #uuid "e524c8b2-8988-581d-b06e-10a171578329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "026cf452-bbce-5666-b06e-eea33f9d7832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489517} :answer-field/id #uuid "44f33cfa-5885-5a2f-90fc-d69e3d361204" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a635937b-674b-54dd-a770-48fe7b870be8" :question/math-academy-id "q-54293" :question/problem "![](images/71/716836dd3dfc93ac26bf9a960d5edcd04da96907b98c39e926a917b7b8feeeee.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nI. $y=-2x+\\frac{1}{2}$\n\nII. $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2$\n\nIII. $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,2)$ and $(4,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/2b/2b9174d86d123228ae24fffeda4d2e4741e4a776f6671f7eff38340a59ba4d40.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y=-2x+\\frac{1}{2}.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,2)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2) & =-1(0)+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2x+\\frac{1}{2}$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,2)$ and $B(4,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0)+2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(4)+2 \\\\ 0 & =-2+2 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,$y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,2)$ and $B(4,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2) & =\\frac{1}{2}(0)+2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =\\frac{1}{2}(4)+2 \\\\ 0 & =2+2 \\\\ 0 & =4\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that represented the line in the graph was $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2,$ corresponding to answer choice II. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490291 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f4f3252-d422-5b2b-87b7-6916c51a12a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489550} {:answer/id #uuid "38679d57-742c-5817-a31c-101bf33ca332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489640} {:answer/id #uuid "685aeab1-eebd-534e-8e4a-d21630e148b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489763} {:answer/id #uuid "a111be60-4cf1-5eab-b12f-bf275dc291fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489871} {:answer/id #uuid "f9fd167a-76c2-572d-ac6f-8214ea48c14a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f4f3252-d422-5b2b-87b7-6916c51a12a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489550} :answer-field/id #uuid "29e6c0ec-30d3-5bc5-89e9-08b3f00c0259" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "acf0d12e-cf58-5db7-b66f-2999c47f2d4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-71829" :question/problem "![](images/c3/c33968d9b5c86e39ba35c88a5f28bb8950178004c763a5c011633b9a060e98a8.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=4x+2.$" :question/worked-solution "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-1,-2)$, $B(0,1)$ and $C(1,6).$\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-1,-2)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4x+2 \\\\ (-2) & =4(-1)+2 \\\\ -2 & =-2\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(0,1)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4x+2 \\\\ (1) & =4(0)+2 \\\\ 1 & =2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(1,6)$ results in true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4x+2 \\\\ (6) & =4(1)+2 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $C$ lie on the line."}]}
